functions.doc:
Stop the execution of the configuration script and alter the implicit action which is done afterwards.

``` c
onreply_route {
    if(status=="200") {
        drop(); # this works
    }
}

onreply_route[FOOBAR] {
    if(status=="200") {
        drop(); # this is ignored
    }
}
```

Stop the execution of the configuration script -- it has the same behaviour as return(0). It does not affect the implicit action to be taken after script execution.

``` c
request_route {
    if (route(ABC)) {
    xlog("L_NOTICE","method $rm is INVITE\n");
    } else {
    xlog("L_NOTICE","method is $rm\n");
    }
}

route[ABC] {
    if (is_method("INVITE")) {
    return(1);
    } else if (is_method("REGISTER")) {
    return(-1);
    } else if (is_method("MESSAGE")) {
    sl_send_reply("403","IM not allowed");
    exit;
    }
}
```

Force to send the message from the specified socket (it **must** be one of the sockets specified with the `listen` directive). If the protocol doesn't match (e.g. UDP message "forced" to a TCP socket) the closest socket of the same protocol is used.

``` c
force_send_socket(10.10.10.10:5060);
force_send_socket(udp:10.10.10.10:5060);
```

Forward in stateless mode the SIP request to destination address set in `$du` or `$ru`.

``` c
$du = "sip:10.0.0.10:5060;transport=tcp";
forward();
```

Test if a flag is set for current processed message (if the flag value is 1). The value of the parameter can be in range of 0..31.

``` c
if(isflagset(3)) {
    log("flag 3 is set\n");
};
```

Checks if a pseudo variable argument contains integer value.

``` c
if(is_int("$avp(foobar)")) {
    log("foobar contains an integer\n");
}
```

Write text message to standard error terminal or syslog. You can specify the log level (the integer id) as first parameter.

``` c
log("just some text message\n");
log(1, "another text message\n");
```

The return() function allows you to return any integer value from a called route() block. You can test the value returned by a route using `$retcode` variable (which is same as `$rc` or `$?`).

``` c
request_route {
    if (route(RET)) {
        xlog("L_NOTICE","method $rm is INVITE\n");
    } else {
        xlog("L_NOTICE","method $rm is REGISTER\n");
    };
}

route[RET] {
    if (is_method("INVITE")) {
        return(1);
    } else if (is_method("REGISTER")) {
        return(-1);
    } else {
        return(0);
    };
}
```

Like `set_forward_no_connect()`, but for replies to the current message (local generated replies and replies forwarded by tm). The behavior depends in which route block the function is called:

``` c
      route[4] {
        //requests from local users. There are usually behind NAT so it does not make sense to try
        //to establish a new TCP connection for the replies
        set_reply_no_connect();
        // do authentication and call routing
        ...
      }
```

Like `set_reply_no_connect`, but closes the TCP connection after sending. Can be used in same route blocks as `set_reply_no_connect`.

``` c
      route {
        ...
        if (...caller-is-not-registered...) {
          // reject unregistered client
          // if request was received via TCP/TLS close the connection, as
          // this may trigger re-registration of the client.
          set_reply_close();
          sl_send_reply("403","REGISTER first");
          exit;
        }
        ...
      }
```

params.doc:
Specify an address to listen (bind) to, a simplified alternative to `listen` paramter that allows specifying the attributes using a structure style.

``` c
socket = {
    attr1 = value1;
    ...
    attrN = valueN;
}
```

Kamailio has built-in support for statistics counter. This means, these counters can be increased, decreased, read and cleared. The statistics counter are defined either by the core (e.g. tcp counters), by modules (e.g. 2xx_transactions by "tmx" module) or by the script writer using the "statistics" module.

``` c
modparam("statistics", "variable", "NOTIFY")

(if method == "NOTIFY") {
    update_stat("NOTIFY", "+1");
}

xlog("Number of received NOTIFYs: $stat(NOTIFY)");
```

Set the name of xavp from where to take Via header field: address and port. Use them to build local Via header.

``` c
xavp_via_fields="customvia"

request_route {
  ...
  $xavp(customvia=>address) = "1.2.3.4";
  $xavp(customvia[0]=>port) = "5080";  # must be string
  via_use_xavp_fields("1");
  t_relay();
}
```

pvars.doc:
**$branch(name)** - reference to attribute 'name' of a branch

``` c
$var(i)=0;
while($var(i)<$branch(count))
{
   xlog("$(branch(uri)[$var(i)])\n");
   $var(i) = $var(i) + 1;
}
```

**$def(name)** - return a defined value.

``` c
#!define ABC xyz

xlog("ABC: $def(ABC)\n");
```

**$defn(name)** - return a defined value as a signed integer.

``` c
#!define FLT_ACC 1

xlog("FLT_ACC: $defn(FLT_ACC)\n");
```

**$defs(name)** - return string quoted defined value. If the defined value is enclosed between single or double quotes, then the value is returned as it is.

``` c
#!define ABC xyz

xlog("ABC: $defs(ABC)\n");
```

**$defv(name)** - return unquoted defined value. Works for both single and double quotes. If the defined value is not enclosed between quotes, then the value is returned as it is.

``` c
#!define ABC "xyz"

xlog("ABC: $defv(ABC)\n");
```

**$fsn** - reference to the name of the forced send socket for the SIP message. The name can be assigned to this variable to select a send socket via its name.

``` c
listen=udp:1.2.3.4:5060 name "s1"
...
$fsn = "s1";
...
$fs = "udp:1.2.3.4:5060";
xdbg("name for forced send socket: $fsn\n");
```

**$rc** - reference to returned code by last invoked function

``` c
    lookup("location");
    $var(rc) = $rc;
    if ($var(rc) < 0) {
        t_newtran();
        switch ($var(rc)) {
            case -1:
            case -3:
                send_reply("404", "Not Found");
                exit;
            case -2:
                send_reply("405", "Method Not Allowed");
                exit;
        }
    }
```

**$rdir(key)** - get the direction of the request within dialog.

``` c
if($rdir(name)=="upstream") {
  xlog("request was sent by callee\n");
}
```

**$sbranch(attr)** - class of variables allowing to manage the values of attributes for static branch. The static branch is internal structure that is used by the functions sbranch_push_ruri() and sbranch_append() from **pv** module, enabling more flexibility in updating the R-URI (first) branch attributes as well as extra branches (e.g., for parallel forking).

``` c
sbranch_reset();
$sbranch(uri) = "sip:127.0.0.1:5080";
$sbranch(dst_uri) =  "sip:127.0.0.1:5090";
$sbranch(path) =  "sip:127.0.0.1:5090, sip:127.0.0.1:5094";
$sbranch(send_socket) =  "udp:127.0.0.1:5060";
sbranch_set_ruri();
```

Attributes of received data. The variables must be used inside **event_route\[core:msg-received\]** routing block.

``` c
event_route[core:msg-received] {
  xlog("rcv on $rcv(af)/$rcv(proto): ($rcv(len)) [$rcv(buf)] from [$rcv(srcip):$rcv(srcport)] to [$rcv(rcvip):$rcv(rcvport)]\n");
}
```

The header name of the header field iterator.

``` c
    hf_iterator_start("i1");
    while(hf_iterator_next("i1")) {
        xlog("hdr[$hfitname(i1)] is: $hfitbody(i1)\n");
    }
    hf_iterator_end("i1");
```

The header body of the header field iterator.

``` c
    hf_iterator_start("i1");
    while(hf_iterator_next("i1")) {
        xlog("hdr[$hfitname(i1)] is: $hfitbody(i1)\n");
    }
    hf_iterator_end("i1");
```

The value of the body line iterator.

``` c
    bl_iterator_start("b1");
    while(bl_iterator_next("b1")) {
        xlog("body line: $blitval(b1)");
    }
    bl_iterator_end("b1");
```

**$sndto(name)** - return attributes of the address to where the request is going to be sent (remote socket).

``` c
onsend_route {
  if($snd(ip)=="10.10.10.10")
  {
    ...
  }
}
```

**$sipdump(name)** - return attributes of the message handled in the event_route\[sipdump:msg\].

``` c
event_route[sipdump:msg] {
  if($sipdump(len) > 1024) {
    ...
  }
}
```

**$siptrace(name)** - return attributes of the message handled in the event_route\[siptrace:msg\].

``` c
event_route[siptrace:msg]
{
    if (allow_address("1", "$siptrace(src_hostip)", "0")) {
        return;

    }
    if (compare_ips($siptrace(src_host), "[2001:DB8::1]")) {
        return;
    }
}
```

_$erl_atom(name)_ pseudo variable allows create analog to Erlang atom data type. Erlang atom is a literal, a constant with name. Formatted output pseudo variable atom could be enclosed in single quotes (') if it does not begin with a lower-case letter or if it contains other characters than alphanumeric characters, underscore (\_), or @.

``` c
$erl_atom(A) = "node@host.tld";

xlogl("L_DEBUG","$$erl_atom(A): $erl_atom(A=>format)\n");
```

Compound data type with a variable number of terms. Formally, a list is either the empty list \[\] or consists of one or more elements.

``` c
$erl_atom(E) = "example";
$erl_list(L) = "list";
$erl_list(L) = "of";
$erl_list(L) = $erl_atom(E);

xlogl("L_DEBUG","length(L): $erl_list(L=>length), format(L): $erl_list(L=>format)\n");

# empty list
$erl_tuple(E[*]) = $null;
```

From the Erlang point of view the tuple compound data type with a fixed number of terms. The module implementation of tuple has the same behavior as the list.

``` c
$erl_atom(e) = "error";

$erl_tuple(T) = "badrpc";
$erl_tuple(T) = $erl_atom(e);

xlogl("L_DEBUG","length(T): $erl_tuple(T=>length), format(T): $erl_tuple(T=>format)\n");
```

The value at the current position in the iterator.

``` c
    sht_iterator_start("i1", "h1");
    while(sht_iterator_next("i1")) {
        xlog("h1[$shtitkey(i1)] is: $shtitval(i1)\n");
    }
    sht_iterator_end("i1");
```

Get the key or the value of expired item inside the event_route\[htable:expired:\_table_name\_\].

``` c
event_route[htable:expired:h1] {
  xlog("expired item ($shtrecord(key),$shtrecord(value))\n");
}
```

- used to build the input for uac_send_req() function of UAC module

``` c
$uac_req(method)="OPTIONS";
$uac_req(ruri)="sip:kamailio.org";
$uac_req(furi)="sip:kamailio.org";
$uac_req(turi)="sip:kamailio.org";
$uac_req(evroute) = 1;
uac_req_send();
...
event_route[uac:reply] {
  xlog("request sent to $uac_req(ruri) completed with code: $uac_req(evcode)\n");
}
```

- return the value of fetched item from queue q

``` c
...
mq_add("myq", "$rU", "call from $fU at $Tf");
...
while(mq_fetch("myq"))
{
   xlog("$mqk(myq) - $mqv(myq)\n");
}
